// P3: division guarded by a disequality test.
// d ranges over [-2, 2]; the program divides only when d != 0, so no
// concrete run ever divides by zero.  A plain interval domain cannot exploit
// the "hole" left in d by d != 0 (interval [ -2, 2 ] is unchanged after
// removing the interior point 0), so it still reports a possible division by
// zero.  This is the classic *relational/set-of-intervals* precision loss and
// is documented as an intentional conservativity boundary (false positive).
input d;
var q = 0;
if (d != 0) {
  q = 100 / d;
}
